0013: mark a CCU color clean after buffer->image on A7XX.

Relations: standalone - one hunk at the tail of tu_copy_buffer_to_image()
(src/freedreno/vulkan/tu_clear_blit.cc), no other file touched. It does not
depend on 0004-0012 and does not change what they decide: it only fixes the
visibility of the writes they already produce.

Why. On A7XX the writes of CP_BLIT land in the CCU, and nothing in the public
copy commands makes them visible to a later reader:

  - r2d_teardown() emits no cache operation at all ("nothing to do");
  - vkCmdPipelineBarrier outside a renderpass does not emit anything either:
    tu_barrier() only ORs bits into cmd->state.cache.flush_bits, and those
    bits are executed at the next natural flush point (start of a renderpass,
    a dispatch, the setup of the next blit, tu_EndCommandBuffer);
  - tu6_emit_flushes() deliberately excludes the clean for a blit
    destination: DST_FLUSH(CCU_COLOR) takes ALL_CLEAN & ~CCU_CLEAN_COLOR
    (tu_cmd_buffer.cc:5847), because a blit does not read through the CCU.

Turnip already knows the rule and applies it on the paths it wrote earlier:

  "On A7XX, blit cache flushes are required to ensure blit writes are visible
   via UCHE. This isn't necessary on A6XX, all writes should be visible
   implicitly"                                            (tu6_emit_flushes())

store_3d_blit() and the tu_copy_image_to_image() staging path emit
FD_CCU_CLEAN_COLOR for exactly this reason. The public copy commands were the
ones left out, so the hole is narrow but real: a buffer->image followed by a
blit reader (image->buffer, image->image) in the same submit reads pre-copy
memory. Shader readers are already covered by the barrier rules
(SRC_FLUSH(CCU_COLOR) on the writer plus DST_FLUSH(UCHE) on the reader); a
blit reader is the one case that is not.

Why this form and not the others (all measured, tests/rp6-vkd3d-sparse-test.c,
94 checks, driver built from the 0001-0012 stack; the matrix is in
results/test-b2isync-{0..15}.log, analysis in docs/analysis.md
section 21.11):

  form                                   result          cost
  no fix (stock)                         19 MISMATCH     -
  clean + WFI per copy                   94/94           one WFI per copy,
                                                        inside a sequence of
                                                        copies where the next
                                                        copy is already
                                                        ordered after the
                                                        previous one
  clean + CACHE_INVALIDATE (+/- WFI)     94/94           two events per copy,
  per copy                                              invalidate is not
                                                        needed: the writer's
                                                        clean is what is
                                                        missing, not the
                                                        reader's view
  CCU_CLEAN_BLIT_CACHE (+/- invalidate)  19 MISMATCH     wrong aperture: r2d
                                                        writes to sysmem
                                                        through CCU color,
                                                        the blit cache is a
                                                        different thing
  deferred flag, flush in the next blit  94/94           a state flag, a hook
                                                        in every blit setup,
                                                        and still no clean if
                                                        the next command is a
                                                        dispatch or the end
                                                        of the command buffer
  lazy bits (this patch)                 94/94, 3x       nothing per copy: the
                                                        next natural flush
                                                        point emits one clean
                                                        + WFI for the whole
                                                        group, and a command
                                                        buffer that ends here
                                                        merges into the CCU
                                                        clean that
                                                        tu_EndCommandBuffer()
                                                        already emits

The last row is also the reason the change is two lines: the machinery for
pending flushes already exists, and every flush point already calls
tu_emit_cache_flush(), so there is nothing to execute here and no new state.

Why not "fix the model" (a separate blit-write access bit that makes the
DST_FLUSH rule include the clean). That is the architecturally right form for
an upstream merge and it is not more expensive - the same pending-bit
machinery, the same point of execution; only a sloppy *_INCOHERENT_WRITE form
would be. It is not the form here because it changes barrier semantics for
every user of the driver at once, while the bug is confined to one command
that forgot to say anything. For an upstream MR the model plus a CTS
reproduction is the better submission; this patch is the smallest correct
change for the stack we run.

What is measured. Probe U/T in tests/rp6-vkd3d-sparse-test.c: fill with
vkCmdCopyBufferToImage, read back with vkCmdCopyImageToBuffer, byte compare,
91-94 checks depending on the probe set. Stock: 19 mismatches, all in the
BC/geometry cases (zero windows that drift between runs - the reader starts
before the CCU has drained). With the patch: 94 EXACT, 0 MISMATCH, stable
over three repeats (results/test-0013-final.log, sha256 e7a33d2d76b0). The
full stack 0001-0014 with no instrumentation gives the same 94/94
(results/test-0014-ship.log, sha256 c17d02bcaae9).

Not covered, deliberately:

  - the symmetric chain: vkCmdCopyImageToBuffer also writes through the blit
    path, so a later blit reading that buffer in the same submit needs the
    same treatment. Readback to the host is not affected (the submit
    boundary flushes everything), which is why it is left for a separate
    change with its own test;
  - a6xx: the writes are visible implicitly there, and the code is guarded by
    if constexpr (CHIP >= A7XX), so nothing is emitted for older chips.

No regressions: the same binary passes all 94 checks, and the patch adds no
command to any submit that does not already end in a flush point.

--- a/src/freedreno/vulkan/tu_clear_blit.cc
+++ b/src/freedreno/vulkan/tu_clear_blit.cc
@@ -3059,6 +3059,36 @@ tu_copy_buffer_to_image(struct tu_cmd_buffer *cmd,
    }
 
    ops->teardown(cmd, cs);
+
+   if constexpr (CHIP >= A7XX) {
+      /* On A7XX the writes of CP_BLIT land in the CCU and are not guaranteed
+       * visible to a reader until the CCU has been cleaned.  Nothing here does
+       * that: r2d_teardown() emits no cache operation, and a pipeline barrier
+       * outside a renderpass only records pending bits in cache->flush_bits,
+       * which are executed at the next natural flush point.  A reader later in
+       * the same submit therefore sees memory as it was before the copy.  The
+       * driver already knows the rule ("On A7XX, blit cache flushes are
+       * required to ensure blit writes are visible via UCHE",
+       * tu6_emit_flushes()) and applies it in store_3d_blit() and in the
+       * tu_copy_image_to_image() staging path; the public copy commands are
+       * what was left out.
+       *
+       * Mark the clean instead of emitting it: the next natural flush point
+       * pays for it, so a group of consecutive copies costs one clean instead
+       * of one per copy, and a command buffer that ends here merges into the
+       * CCU clean that tu_EndCommandBuffer() emits anyway.  WAIT_FOR_IDLE
+       * travels with it because the event write is asynchronous and only the
+       * wait makes a following reader start after the clean has landed; at a
+       * flush point the wait costs nothing, and carrying it in the same
+       * bitmask keeps the pair together across command buffer boundaries.
+       *
+       * Not covered by this: the symmetric chain - an image->buffer copy also
+       * writes through the blit path, so a later blit reading that buffer in
+       * the same submit needs the same treatment.  That case is left alone.
+       */
+      cmd->state.cache.flush_bits |= TU_CMD_FLAG_CCU_CLEAN_COLOR |
+                                     TU_CMD_FLAG_WAIT_FOR_IDLE;
+   }
 }
 
 template <chip CHIP>
